course: dictionary
lesson: 2168
title: A grim word with a serious meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Corpse is a grim word. It means the dead body of a person, and sometimes of an animal. Writers use it when they want to tell the reader that someone has died, and the mood is serious and sad.
explain: Context gives you clues. «The titan arum, or corpse flower, smells of rotting meat to call carrion beetles and flies.» Rotting meat and carrion are things that have died and are decaying, so the word links with death, even though this sentence is about a plant.
explain: Theatre actors use corpse in a different way. To corpse means to burst out laughing by accident when you should be serious. Always check: swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A corpse is the dead body of a person, and sometimes of an animal.
fact: Gentler words for a dead body are «body» and «remains».
fact: «As pale as a corpse» means very pale, as if the person looks dead.
fact: The titan arum is called the corpse flower because it smells of rotting meat.
fact: In UK theatre slang, «to corpse» means to burst out laughing by accident while acting.
exercise: «As pale as a corpse» means very pale, as if someone looks dead. What does corpse mean in that phrase? | A kind of flower | *A dead body | A long, thin pencil | A sunny field
exercise: Which is a gentler word for a dead body? | Bicycle | *Remains | Thunder | Lunch
exercise: Leo says «to corpse» means to fall asleep on stage. What does it really mean? | To cry during a sad play | *To burst out laughing by accident while acting | To fall asleep on stage | To hide backstage
test: «The titan arum, or corpse flower, smells of rotting meat to call carrion beetles and flies.» What do the clues rotting meat and carrion beetles suggest? | A happy party | A soft pillow | A sunny day | *Death and decay
test: Which phrase uses corpse to mean very pale? | *As pale as a corpse | Bright as the sun | Red as a rose | Pale as milk
exam: In a mystery story, what does «corpse» tell the reader? | A funny joke | A sunny field | A kind of flower | *Someone has died
